{\color{blue}The controlled comparisons fix $\lambda_1=\lambda_2=1$, RBF bandwidth parameter $\gamma=0.003$, 256 requested landmarks, batch size 256, Adam learning rate 0.01, and 64 epochs, on an Intel Xeon Platinum 8452Y host with one computational thread (Python 3.10.9, NumPy 2.2.6, and scikit-learn 1.7.2). These common settings are not claimed optimal. Unless varied explicitly, loss coefficients are $C_S=1000/(2n_S)$ and $C_T=1000/(2n_T)$, so both domains receive equal total coefficient mass and $n_SC_S+n_TC_T=1000$. Target-only fitting uses $C_T=1000/n_T$, centered target residual variance, no projected discrepancy, and its own fitting statistics and landmarks. Its epochs are raised to the smallest integer giving at least as many Adam updates as the paired full model; equal update opportunities do not certify equal optimization accuracy. The full, no-variance, no-discrepancy, and target-only variants use seeds 0, 1, and 2. These are algorithmic repeats on a shared test period, not independent datasets or confidence intervals.}

{\color{blue}Point prediction uses $\tau=0.5$. Interval fits use $\tau_L=0.025$ and $\tau_U=0.975$. Define ordered endpoints $\ell(x)=\min\{f_{\tau_L}(x),f_{\tau_U}(x)\}$ and $u(x)=\max\{f_{\tau_L}(x),f_{\tau_U}(x)\}$. Calibration scores are $s_i=\max\{\ell(x_i)-y_i,y_i-u(x_i),0\}$. With $\alpha=0.05$, set $k=\lceil(n_{\rm cal}+1)(1-\alpha)\rceil$, $q=s_{(k)}$, and report $[\ell(x)-q,u(x)+q]$. A finite interval requires $k\le n_{\rm cal}$. On Farm~6, $n_{\rm cal}=225$ and $k=215$. Test labels do not set the expansion. This is an empirical temporal evaluation of a split-calibration rule, without an exchangeability or conditional-coverage guarantee \citep{romano2019cqr}. The historical diagnostic instead used raw levels 0.2 and 0.8; its 95\% value describes the calibration target, not the uncalibrated quantile span.}

{\color{blue}Each branch saves source/target fitting identifiers, preprocessing statistics, landmarks, model coefficients, calibration scores, and individual test predictions. Code and workbook hashes accompany the fixed protocol. Reported fitting time includes landmark selection, feature construction, regularizer construction, and Adam, but excludes workbook loading, preprocessing, prediction, and post-fit objective diagnostics. Timings are wall-clock observations on the shared compute host, not deployment latency or a hardware-independent complexity claim.}

